WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
Cloud deployment scripts often apply more than hand-written commands.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:16.440
They may deploy Infrastructure-as-Code templates, container images, packages, configuration bundles, rendered manifests, generated release files, or environment overlays.

3
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:21.500
The reviewed source is not always the same as the effective change.

4
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:30.320
Parameters, variables, feature flags, release metadata, or generated files can transform a simple input into a different deployment outcome.

5
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:36.120
Secure deployment scripts make the effective change visible before high-impact apply steps.

6
00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:49.180
Depending on the toolchain, that visibility may come from plans, diffs, previews, rendered templates, package metadata, image digests, release manifests, or approval records.

7
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.280
The point is to connect what reviewers saw to what the automation actually applied.

8
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.860
A deployment should not become a blind apply of generated content that nobody can explain.

9
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.860
Artifacts should be versioned and traceable.

10
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:10.880
A release should identify which source revision, package, image, configuration bundle, or rendered output moved into the environment.

11
00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:23.220
Provenance and integrity controls can be discussed defensively as a way to preserve trust in the deployment path: know where the artifact came from, who approved it, and whether it is the same artifact promoted through the workflow.

12
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.500
Review should focus on the effective resource changes and operational impact.

13
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:39.120
Does the deployment broaden exposure, change identity permissions, alter routing, update storage, weaken logging, change encryption expectations, or modify data-handling behavior?

14
00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:45.700
Secure helpers should preserve this context so reviewers can make informed decisions before production changes happen.
